  "problem": "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.11)^{5}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n$(1.11)^{5}\u2248$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot {x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{5} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}+\u22ef\\,+{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\u22481+5x+10{x}^{2}+10{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.11)^{5} & =(1+0.11)^{5} \\\\  & \u22481+5(0.11)+10(0.11)^{2}+10(0.11)^{3} \\\\  & =1+5(0.11)+10(0.012\\,1)+10(0.001\\,331) \\\\  & =1+0.55+0.121+0.013\\,31 \\\\  & \u22481.684,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
